<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/13/apple-homeview-lineup-261013-1791896790884-952455c0a1.jpg" alt="Source: Apple."><p class="image-caption">Source: Apple.</p></div>
<p id="p2">Today, Apple <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/10/meet-apple-homeview-a-new-way-to-enjoy-entertainment-and-manage-the-home/" rel="noopener noreferrer">announced the highly anticipated HomeView</a>, a Siri AI-powered device for controlling your HomeKit devices and accessing other apps and services like Apple Music, your calendar, Reminders, Apple Podcasts, and more.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/13/apple-homeview-screen-angles-261013-1791896798683-233c17ac86.jpg" alt="The table top HomeView's screen tilts. Source: Apple."><p class="image-caption">The table top HomeView’s screen tilts. Source: Apple.</p></div>
<p id="p4">HomeView comes in two configurations, each of which is $299 and will launch on November 13 and can be preordered today. The first sits on a flat surface and has a domed aluminum base and a 7-inch Liquid Retina True Tone display that tilts. The base houses a downward-firing woofer and two force-cancelling passive radiators and the display has two drivers that support Spatial Audio.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/13/apple-homeview-wall-mounted-261013-1791897964169-aed90f5a8a.jpg" alt="The Wall Mounted HomeView. Source: Apple."><p class="image-caption">The Wall Mounted HomeView. Source: Apple.</p></div>
<p id="p6">The second HomeView model is meant to be hung on a wall, but the functionality of both is roughly the same. The main difference being that the wall-mounted version lacks the other model’s base and its woofer.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/13/apple-homeview-collection-of-faces-261013-1791897978717-cdc4a93e06.jpg" alt="HomeView faces. Source: Apple."><p class="image-caption">HomeView faces. Source: Apple.</p></div>
<p id="p8">The HomeView features a series of customizable faces that are navigated by swipe gestures. There are faces for photos, weather, a clock, for example, which are reminiscent of Apple Watch faces. There are also tiles for controlling HomeKit devices and widgets for things like the weather and your calendar, which are similar to the iPhone’s StandBy Mode widgets.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/13/apple-homeview-cooking-261013-1791896804330-eb3167e2e8.jpg" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p10">As for apps, Apple includes a selection of their own apps like FaceTime, Music, Podcasts, TV, News, and Messages along with select video and audio apps from third parties. As a smart home hub, HomeView allows users to watch HomeKit camera feeds and control locks, thermostats, lights, blinds, and other compatible devices. Of course, Siri AI is a big part of the HomeView story, too, serving as a means for navigating the device’s UI, interacting with apps, and requesting information conversationally.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/13/apple-homeview-lifestyle-living-room-261013-1791896795330-7760f07924.jpg" alt="Source: Apple."><p class="image-caption">Source: Apple.</p></div>
<p id="p12">Under the hood, the HomeView is powered by an A18 chip with Secure Exclaves for processing audio and video data. <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/10/meet-apple-homeview-a-new-way-to-enjoy-entertainment-and-manage-the-home/" rel="noopener noreferrer">According to Apple</a>, Messages and Calendar content are only available when a user is recognized and their paired iPhone or iPad is present and recently unlocked.</p>
<p id="p13">Overall, I love the look of the HomeView and have been thinking about where I’d place it in my home as I write this. I also like the blend of concepts that draw from the Apple Watch, StandBy Mode, and widgets. My hope, though, is that the third-party developer story expands beyond the limited audio and video apps mentioned in Apple’s press release.</p>
<p id="p14"><a href="https://www.apple.com/shop/buy-home/apple-homeview" rel="noopener noreferrer">Preorders are live now</a> in Australia, Canada, India, New Zealand, the UK, and the U.S. and will begin on Thursday, October 15, at 9 a.m. SGT in Singapore. The HomeView is not available in the EU because Siri AI is not available there.</p>
<p id="p15">For the wall-mounted version, Apple is offering a $59.95 USB-C Wall Connector and a $79.95 AC Wired option, both of which are available for preorder now in the U.S. A Power Mount PoE option will be available in early 2027 for $129.95 in the U.S., too.</p>
